        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A cinder block basement is not waterproofed by the product you choose. It is waterproofed by the order you do things in: get surface water away from the wall, find out whether the cores are holding water, then pick the fix that matches what the wall is actually doing.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a damp wall with dry cores, two properly prepped coats of masonry waterproofer are cheap and effective. For full cores or a leaking floor joint, spend the money on drainage instead of paint. And for a bowing wall, spend it on an engineer first. Everything else is detail.
          </p>
